      en: "El Infiernito, located within the Monquirá Archaeological Park near Villa de Leyva, is an intriguing pre-Columbian site built by the Muisca people. It is most famous for its 'solar field', which contains dozens of large, phallic-shaped stone columns, some standing several meters tall. Archaeologists believe these columns served as an astronomical observatory, used by the Muisca to track the seasons and determine the best times for planting and harvesting by observing the shadows they cast. The site also includes several burial mounds and underground tombs. The name 'El Infiernito' (Little Hell) was given by Spanish conquistadors who were shocked by the phallic symbolism and considered it a place of pagan worship. Despite its name, the site was a sophisticated center of science and spirituality for the Muisca culture. Its location in a beautiful, arid valley with views of the surrounding mountains adds to its mysterious and ancient allure."
